"Shows the actors involved in a service and the distinct value, material or information exchanges that connect them.\n\nUse when: Find missing partners or unsupported exchanges in a service. Understand who contributes to and benefits from service delivery.\n\nAvoid when: The question is formal reporting authority or the sequence of one service journey.\n\nVisual grammar: Actor nodes identify people, organizations or service roles. Typed directed connections show the giving and receiving of value. Separate layers distinguish money, information and material exchanges.\n\nBuild: Define the service boundary and focal user's situation. Identify visible and backstage actors and their contributions. Document exchanges and split dense relationship types into separate views.\n\nCheck: Direction identifies the actual giver and receiver for each exchange. Relationship types have a clear legend. Preserve real exchanges through layered views instead of removing crossings selectively.\n\nPitfalls: Treating proximity as an unexplained influence score. Drawing untyped lines that conceal what actors exchange.\n\nExample: A food-delivery ecosystem separates customer payments, kitchen preparation, courier transport and status information to expose coordination gaps.\n\nAlternatives: Stakeholder onion map, BPMN collaboration."
